# {{name}}

A Lunibee bot, written in TypeScript. Created with `lunibee init`.

Commands and events are found automatically: add a file under `src/commands` or
`src/events` and restart.
{{#slash}}
Slash commands are registered with Discord on every start, so there is no separate
deploy step.
{{/slash}}
{{#message}}
Message commands start with the prefix (`!` by default, `PREFIX` in `.env`), such as `!ping`.
{{/message}}

## Prerequisites

- [Bun](https://bun.sh) 1.4 or newer. Lunibee runs on Bun: the scripts, development
  and the compiled bot (`bun dist/index.js`) all use it. npm or pnpm can install the
  dependencies, but not run the bot.
- A Discord application with a bot user

## Installation

```sh
bun install
```

## Environment

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill it in. `.env` is ignored by git.

| Variable | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `DISCORD_TOKEN` | yes | The bot's token. |
{{#slash}}
| `CLIENT_ID` | yes | The application ID, used to register commands. |
| `GUILD_ID` | no | A server ID: commands are registered there only and update at once. Leave empty for global commands. |
{{/slash}}
{{#message}}
| `PREFIX` | no | The text message commands start with. Default `!`. |
{{/message}}
| `NODE_ENV` | no | `development` (default), `production` or `test`. |
{{#message}}
| `MESSAGE_CONTENT` | no | `true` (default) to read message text in servers, which message commands need. |
{{/message}}
{{^message}}
| `MESSAGE_CONTENT` | no | `true` to read message text in servers. Off by default. |
{{/message}}

The configuration is checked at startup (`src/config.ts`); a missing or invalid
value stops the bot with a message saying what to fix. The token is never logged.

## Setting up the application

1. Open the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications) and create an application.
{{#slash}}
2. **General Information:** copy the *Application ID* into `CLIENT_ID`.
{{/slash}}
3. **Bot:** *Reset Token* and copy it into `DISCORD_TOKEN`.
{{#message}}
   Under *Privileged Gateway Intents*, turn on *Message Content Intent*.
{{/message}}
4. **OAuth2 → URL Generator:** tick `bot`{{#slash}} and `applications.commands`{{/slash}}, pick the
   permissions the bot needs (for `ping`, *Send Messages*), and open the URL to
   add the bot to your server.
{{#slash}}
5. To develop against one server, turn on *Developer Mode* in Discord's settings,
   right-click the server, *Copy Server ID*, and put it in `GUILD_ID`.
{{/slash}}

## Development

```sh
bun run dev
```

Runs `src/index.ts` and restarts when a file changes.

## Production

```sh
bun run build   # compiles src/ to dist/
bun run start   # runs dist/index.js
```

`bun run typecheck` checks the types without writing files.

{{#slash}}
## Command registration

On every start, `src/services/register.ts` sends every command found under
`src/commands` to Discord, replacing the previous set:

- with `GUILD_ID`, to that server only (changes appear at once);
- without it, globally, for every server the bot is in.

If Discord refuses the commands, the bot stops with the reason instead of running
with outdated commands. Network errors and rate limits are retried first. Sending the
whole set on each start is deliberate: it replaces what Discord has, so a renamed or
deleted command never lingers. In development, `bun run dev` restarts on every change
and registers the new set.

{{/slash}}
`lunibee check` loads every command and event the way startup does (without logging
in) and reports invalid modules and duplicate names, and `.env` keys missing from
`.env.example`.

{{#slash}}
{{#message}}
`/ping` and `!ping` run the same file: `execute` answers the slash command,
`executeMessage` the message command.

{{/message}}
{{/slash}}
## Adding a command

Create a file anywhere under `src/commands` (folders are categories), for example
`src/commands/fun/roll.ts`:

```ts
{{#slash}}
import { CreateSlashCommand } from "lunibee";
{{/slash}}
import { defineCommand } from "../../types/index.js";

export default defineCommand({
{{#slash}}
    data: new CreateSlashCommand().setName("roll").setDescription("Rolls a die."),
    async execute({ interaction }) {
        await interaction.reply(`You rolled ${1 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 6)}.`);
    },
{{/slash}}
{{^slash}}
    name: "roll",
    description: "Rolls a die.",
{{/slash}}
{{#message}}
    async executeMessage({ message }) {
        await message.reply(`You rolled ${1 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 6)}.`);
    },
{{/message}}
});
```

Restart the bot: it is loaded{{#slash}} and registered{{/slash}}. `bunx lunibee create command roll --category fun`
writes the same file for you. Names must be unique; two commands
with the same name stop the startup. Files and folders starting with `_` are skipped.

## Adding an event

Create a file anywhere under `src/events`, for example `src/events/guild/guildCreate.ts`:

```ts
import { defineEvent } from "../../types/index.js";
import { logger } from "../../utils/logger.js";

export default defineEvent({
    name: "guildCreate",
    async execute({ client }, guild) {
        logger.info(`Joined ${guild.name}.`);
    },
});
```

`name` is the event; its arguments are typed from it. Add `once: true` to run only
the first time. A handler that throws is logged and the bot keeps running.

## Project layout

```
src/
{{#slash}}
├── index.ts          startup: config → commands → registration → events → login
{{/slash}}
{{^slash}}
├── index.ts          startup: config → commands → events → login
{{/slash}}
├── config.ts         typed, validated settings from .env
├── commands/         {{#slash}}slash{{/slash}}{{#message}}{{#slash}} and {{/slash}}message{{/message}} commands, found recursively
├── events/           event handlers, found recursively
├── handlers/         command and event discovery
{{#slash}}
├── services/         command registration
{{/slash}}
├── types/            shared types and defineCommand / defineEvent
└── utils/logger.ts   logging (secrets are redacted)
```

## Troubleshooting

- **"Invalid configuration"**: a variable in `.env` is missing or malformed; the
  message names it. Check that `.env` is in the project root.
{{#slash}}
- **"Could not register … command(s)"**: `CLIENT_ID` does not belong to the token's
  application, the bot was not added with the `applications.commands` scope, or
  `GUILD_ID` is a server the bot is not in.
- **Commands do not appear**: global commands can take a while to show up in
  Discord's client; set `GUILD_ID` while developing.
{{/slash}}
- **Login fails with an authentication error**: the token is wrong or was reset;
  copy a new one into `DISCORD_TOKEN`.
- **Message text is empty**{{#message}} or `!ping` does not answer{{/message}}: set `MESSAGE_CONTENT=true` and enable the *Message
  Content* intent in the Developer Portal.

